# Summary
Introduced denial-of-service risk: invalid or huge expiration dates in POST policies can panic or spin the server during date_to_epoch conversion.
The newly added POST policy validation parses ISO-8601 dates manually and converts them to epoch seconds without validating the ranges of year/month/day. The conversion loop iterates from 1970 to the provided year and indexes a fixed month-day table using the unvalidated month. A policy expiration such as month 14 triggers an out-of-bounds index panic, and an extremely large year forces a massive loop. Because the server processes requests serially and does not catch panics, an authenticated POST Object request with a malicious policy can crash the process or consume CPU.

# Reviewer note
This report was valid.

It was fixed in commit `2afedac` (`fix: harden post policy expiration parsing`), which tightened ISO-8601 expiration parsing, rejected invalid month/day/time and oversized years, rejected pre-epoch dates, and replaced the unchecked epoch conversion with a bounded checked conversion path plus regression tests.

That fix was later folded into the broader parser-hardening work. Commit `3731c23` (`auth: share iso8601 utc parser logic`) moved the risky ISO-8601 UTC parsing and epoch conversion into a shared helper used by both POST policy expiration and Object Lock XML/header parsing, so the original bug fix now sits on the common date-parsing path rather than a one-off local implementation.

