# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
The issue is a real availability bug on the main S3 POST Object path (validate_post_policy -> parse_iso8601/date_to_epoch) and can crash or stall the single-threaded server. However, exploitation requires valid credentials to sign the policy and has no confidentiality/integrity impact, so medium severity is appropriate rather than high/critical.
## Likelihood
high - Requires valid access key/secret to sign the malicious policy; unauthenticated users cannot reach the vulnerable path, but any credentialed user can trigger it with a single request. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - A crafted authenticated POST policy can panic the date parser or consume CPU in the conversion loop, crashing or stalling the single-threaded server process (availability impact only).
## Assumptions
- Service is deployed with ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR reachable by the attacker (e.g., bound to 0.0.0.0 or exposed behind a proxy).
- Attacker possesses valid access key/secret or otherwise can sign a POST policy to pass authenticate_post.
- Valid access key/secret to sign a POST policy (SigV2 form auth)
- Ability to send a POST Object multipart/form-data request with a policy field
## Path
n1 -> n2 -> n3 -> n4 -> n5
## Path evidence
- `crates/server/src/http/mod.rs:412-490` - POST Object request handling parses multipart form and calls validate_post_policy on the policy field.
- `crates/auth/src/post.rs:235-266` - parse_iso8601/date_to_epoch lack range checks and index month_days by month, enabling panic or long loops with crafted dates.
- `crates/auth/src/post.rs:17-61` - authenticate_post requires access key, policy, and signature, indicating the exploit requires valid credentials.
- `crates/server/src/config.rs:18-38` - listen address is configurable (default 127.0.0.1:9000), informing exposure assumptions.
- `crates/server/src/main.rs:83-99` - HTTP server runs in a serial request loop, so a panic or long loop affects the whole service.
## Narrative
POST Object handling parses multipart form data and, when a policy field is present, calls validate_post_policy (server/src/http/mod.rs:412-490). The expiration parser parse_iso8601 accepts year/month/day without range checks and passes them to date_to_epoch, which loops from 1970..year and indexes month_days[m as usize] for m in 1..month (auth/src/post.rs:235-266). An invalid month (e.g., >12) can trigger an out-of-bounds panic, and an extremely large year can cause excessive CPU use. Because the server runs a single-threaded request loop (server/src/main.rs:83-99), a panic or long loop can crash or stall the service. Exploitation requires a valid access key/secret to sign the POST policy (auth/src/post.rs:17-61), so the impact is an authenticated availability DoS rather than an auth bypass.
## Controls
- SigV2 POST authentication required for policy validation
- Bucket write authorization check for POST object
## Blindspots
- No deployment manifests were available to confirm whether the service is internet-exposed or protected behind internal-only networking.
- No runtime PoC was executed in this environment; assessment is based on static code analysis only.
- Unknown whether production deployments use panic recovery or supervision to auto-restart the process after a panic.
